#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186327814 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d54bab77-d77f-5275-b454-ddb565eaa280" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Indefinite Integral of a Rational Function Using Partial Fractions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "73cf6ac4-f0f0-5512-a57b-e7378900b295" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327826}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "73cf6ac4-f0f0-5512-a57b-e7378900b295" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327826} :answer-field/id #uuid "85758217-04a2-5aa3-8ddd-e0c46cc59e54" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327825} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f1fea7ea-193d-5611-8661-4485ed158ebf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327828}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f1fea7ea-193d-5611-8661-4485ed158ebf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327828} :answer-field/id #uuid "af6489b1-05a1-55da-9195-4e8d26b2515a" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327827}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5f5dc83b-4d6f-5a90-9b27-05db05e5bf2b" :question/math-academy-id "q-128339" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)=\\frac{7}{2x^2-5x-3}$. Find the coefficients $A$ and $B$ in its partial-fraction decomposition:\n\n$$\\frac{7}{2x^2-5x-3}=\\frac{A}{x-3}+\\frac{B}{2x+1},\\qquad x\\ne3,-\\frac12.$$\n\n$A=$ {{field-1}}, $B=$ {{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "Factor the denominator:\n\n$$2x^2-5x-3=(x-3)(2x+1).$$\n\nMultiplying the partial-fraction identity by $(x-3)(2x+1)$ gives\n\n$$7=A(2x+1)+B(x-3)=(2A+B)x+(A-3B).$$\n\nMatching coefficients yields $2A+B=0$ and $A-3B=7$. Thus $B=-2A$, so $7A=7$ and\n\n$$A=1,\\qquad B=-2.$$\n\nSubstitution verifies the identity because $(2x+1)-2(x-3)=7$.\n\nIf this decomposition is used to evaluate the improper integral from $4$ to infinity, keep a common finite upper bound $R$ and take the limit only after combining the terms:\n\n$$\\int_4^\\infty f(x)\\,dx=\\lim_{R\\to\\infty}\\left(\\int_4^R\\frac{dx}{x-3}-2\\int_4^R\\frac{dx}{2x+1}\\right)=\\lim_{R\\to\\infty}\\ln\\frac{9(R-3)}{2R+1}=\\ln\\frac92.$$\n\nThe two separate improper integrals each diverge, so they cannot be subtracted as independently evaluated improper integrals."}]}
